%n elī-ma | ana ṣūrim | ša šadî
#note: See @bib{RN117@250}: “This line is — at least in part — an imperative counterpart of a narrative couplet in OB Schøyen₂ 27–8: @i{īlīma Gilgāmeš ana ṣēr šadîm} / @i{ittanaplas kalīšunu ḫursānī}. The meaning of @akk{ṣu₂-ri-im} is taken from Semitic cognates, as proposed by @bib{LS304@217}, but the new parallel offers another solution, that @akk{ṣu₂-ri-im} is a spelling mistake for @akk{ṣe-ri-im}. The traces after @i{naplis} were read @akk{a#-na# [eq]-li-im} by @bib{RN2652@100}, but neither copy supports this. Nor does any spelling of @i{ḫursānī} look possible at the end of the line.”
// cf. L I.4 OB "Schøyen₂" 27

%n [il]um | ibrī | ša nellakūšum
#note: As noted by @bib{RN117@247}, here and passim verbs I-ʾ are written with a @i{ne}- sign in the manuscripts from Tell Harmal.
// cf. L I.4 OB "Schøyen₂" 14
// cf. L I.4 OB "Boǧ₂" 21'-22'

%n ul | rīmum-ma || nukkur | mimma
#note: @bib{RN117@251}: “That the first clause is a question is made clear by the new parallel, OB Schøyen₂ 15: @i{ul šadûm-mā nukkur mimma}. In the matter of the last word I follow van Dijk, @i{Sumer} 14, p. 115, who in @i{TIM} IX, p. xi, vigorously rejects the reading @i{mi-im-m}[@i{u-šu}] proposed by @bib{LS304@216}. The latter restoration is ruled out for the following reasons: (a) the break appears to be too small to accept both @i{mu} and @i{šu}; (b) in order to align the ends of lines with the right-hand edge the scribe of this tablet routinely leaves a big gap before the last sign, but never before the last but one: thus only one sign is expected after @i{mi-im}; and (c) the parallels have @i{mimma}, OB Schøyen₂ 15 // 17 and MB Boǧ₂ obv. 23': @i{ul ḫursān} / @i{mimma nuk}[@i{kur}], the latter already noted by @bib{RN2652@116}. Landsberger’s translation of the present line, ‘nicht im mindesten ist der Wildstier widrig’, assumes an unusual word order in which the subject (@i{rīmumma}) splits the predicate (@i{ul nukkur}). Like von Soden I divide the present line into two clauses, with a caesura at their common boundary: @i{úl} | @i{rīmúmma} || @i{núkkur} | @i{mímma}.”
// cf. L I.4 OB "Schøyen₂" 15
// cf. L I.4 OB "Boǧ₂" 21'c-22'a
// cf. L I.4 OB "Boǧ₂" 23'a

%n zīmū | ša tāmuru || šamaš | namrum
#note: @bib{RN117@251}: “Van Dijk and von Soden suggested a reading of the first word as @akk{ri#-mu}. The traces recorded on Lambert’s copy do not concur and I have adopted the reading proposed by J. Renger (@bib{RN168CaRe@96}). Now that the pattern of the dreams is more clearly understood, Renger’s objection to @i{ri-mu}, that in this tablet @i{rīmum} should be written with mimation, is reinforced by the consideration that the wild bull certainly symbolizes Ḫuwawa not Šamaš. Šamaš was well known for his @i{zīmu}, which was proverbially bright; the word became a technical term for the light emitted by the sun and other celestial bodies (see @i{CAD} Z, p. 121, and note Renger’s translation ‘Lichtgestalt’). This and the following line find a parallel in a couplet of Enkidu’s explanation of the avalanche dream (OB Schøyen₂ 21–2): @i{u ša tāmuru Šamaš šarru} / @i{ina ūmi ša dannatim iṣabbat qātka}.”
// cf. L I.4 OB "Nippur" a+6'
// cf. L I.4 OB "Schøyen₂" 21
// cf. L I.4 OB "Schøyen₃" b+7'
// cf. L I.4 MB "Boğ₂" 26'
